  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level3_TheDigitalTrap
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_3
  levelNumber: 3
  displayName: The Digital Trap
  sceneName: Room3_DigitalTrap
  description: Convincing messages in a cybersecurity operations room. Compare each
    one with the evidence before you decide.
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: de67521957d88cb478527ddaa3a59230, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: "LEVEL 3 \u2014 THE DIGITAL TRAP"
  briefingText: 'The traps are getting more convincing. This room has six questions:
    five case questions with four answer choices and one message or web page to judge
    as SCAM or LEGITIMATE. They arrive in a different order on every attempt, and
    the answer choices are shuffled too, so read each one carefully instead of remembering
    positions.'
  caseNotes:
  - 'Trusted domain (case files): examplebank.example'
  objectives:
  - Answer all six questions in this room.
  - Read the whole situation before you choose.
  - Score 720 or more to unlock the next room.
  messages:
  - id: M03
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Multiple Choice
    body: A login page shows a padlock and starts with <b>https://</b>. <b>What does
      the padlock actually prove?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: HTTPS (TLS) protects data <i>in transit</i> so others on the network
      can't read or change it. Certificates are easy for anyone to get for their
      own domain, so scam sites often have padlocks too. The padlock tells you the
      connection is private, not who you're talking to. You still have to check the
      domain.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - The site is owned by the real company
    - Data between you and <i>this</i> site is encrypted, but the site itself could
      still be fake
    - The site has been checked by the government
    - The site cannot contain malware
    correctChoice: 1
    feedback: The padlock means <b>encrypted</b>, not <b>trustworthy</b>. Fake sites
      have padlocks too. Check the domain.
    curriculumRef: "Advanced Cybersecurity Strategies (BIT4233). Supporting: Computer
      Security (BIT1225), Cryptography \u2014 Modern Cryptography (\"secure data
      in transit and at rest\")"
  - id: M04
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Identify the Threat
    body: "<b>SMS from an unknown number:</b> \"Ma, si Migs 'to. Nasira phone ko,
      bagong number ko 'to. Pa-send naman \u20B13,000 sa PayLite 0917 555 0188, emergency
      lang. Wag ka muna tumawag, wala pa akong load.\" <b>What attack is this?</b>"
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'The sender claims to be family on a "new number", invents an emergency,
      asks for money, and <b>blocks you from checking</b> ("don''t call yet"). That
      is social engineering: it exploits trust and urgency rather than technology.
      To check, call Migs''s <i>old</i> number or ask something only the real Migs
      would know.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Impersonation ("new number" family emergency) social engineering
    - Malware infection
    - A data breach
    - A promotional spam message
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: 'Impersonation. New number + emergency + ''don''t call'' means: <b>call
      the old number first.</b>'
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Weeks 10\u201311:
      Cybersecurity Threats and Attack Landscape"
  - id: M05
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: What Should You Do?
    body: During your OJT, you typed your work password into a page you opened from
      an email. A minute later, you notice the address was wrong.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'Fast reporting limits the damage: IT can lock sessions, block the
      site and warn others. Changing the password quickly makes the stolen one useless.
      Hiding the mistake is unprofessional and gives the attacker time. Forwarding
      spreads the dangerous link. Deleting it removes evidence IT needs.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Say nothing, since nothing bad has happened yet
    - Forward the email to coworkers to ask if they got it too
    - Delete the email so no one finds out
    - Change the password through the official site right away, report to IT with
      the email, and watch for login or MFA prompts you didn't start
    correctChoice: 3
    feedback: Report it. Mistakes happen, and <b>hiding them</b> is what makes them
      serious.
    curriculumRef: "Social and Professional Issues (BIT2105). Supporting: IAS Week
      16 (incident reporting procedures) \u2014 Weeks 13\u201314: Security Incidents
      (\"recommend appropriate preventive and corrective measures\")"
  - id: M06
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Multiple Choice
    body: A classmate's school account password was stolen through phishing. <b>Which
      control is most likely to stop the attacker from logging in?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'MFA is a <b>technical control</b>: the attacker needs a second
      factor (an app prompt, code or security key) as well as the password. The other
      choices don''t affect a remote login. MFA is not perfect: only approve prompts
      that you started yourself.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - A stronger Wi-Fi password at home
    - A privacy screen protector
    - Multi-factor authentication (MFA) on the account
    - Clearing the browser history
    correctChoice: 2
    feedback: MFA. A stolen password alone isn't enough, <b>if you never approve
      a prompt you didn't start.</b>
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Week 15:
      Security Controls and Protection Mechanisms (\"administrative, technical, and
      physical controls\")"
  - id: M07
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scenario-Based
    body: "At a caf\xE9 you see two networks: <b>Kape_FreeWiFi</b> and <b>Kape_Free_WiFi</b>.
      After you connect to one, a page says: \"Log in with your SocialBook email
      and password to get internet access.\""
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: Two networks with almost the same name suggests a fake "evil twin"
      hotspot. A network page has no reason to ask for a <i>different</i> site's
      password. That is credential harvesting. <b>A VPN wouldn't help here:</b> it
      encrypts your traffic, but it can't protect a password you type into a fake
      page yourself. Turning off the firewall removes protection.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Don't enter it. Ask the staff for the real network name, and use mobile data
      for anything sensitive
    - "Enter it, since caf\xE9s often ask you to log in"
    - Try both networks until one works without a login
    - Turn off your firewall so the network connects faster
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Fake hotspot. No Wi-Fi network needs your <b>social media password</b>.
      Ask staff, or use mobile data.
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Network Security (\"firewalls,
      intrusion detection/prevention systems (IDS/IPS), and VPNs\")"
  - id: M08
    channel: 0
    sender: ExampleBank
    senderDetail: In the same thread as your real ExampleBank messages
    subject: 
    body: 'ExampleBank: Your account will be suspended TODAY. Update your details
      at'
    link: examplebank-ph.example/update
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 'Trusted domain (case files): examplebank.example'
    explanation: 'Text sender names (sender IDs) can be faked, and a faked one lands
      in the same thread as the real sender''s messages. Where the message appears
      doesn''t prove where it came from. The evidence is in the content: examplebank-ph.example
      isn''t the trusted domain, and it threatens a same-day suspension. Many banks
      have also announced that they no longer send clickable links by text.'
    trigger: 3
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: 'Scam. Sender names can be <b>faked</b>, so the thread proves nothing.
      Check the link: it''s not examplebank.example.'
    curriculumRef: "Cyber Forensics (BIT2226) \u2014 Mobile Device Forensics"
